# Producer preparation: prospective option-resumption construction

Actual author `/root/relational_critic_producer`. Preparation only. No production runner is supplied or executed. No candidate, production semantic parent, canonical file, control manifest or production target is created here. The hand fixture is a manually enumerated calibration program, not one of twelve achieved independent production families.

The final public task semantics and metadata draft followed by amendments r1 and r2 govern a possible later `option-resumption-pilot-v1`. Its 12 families, 108 tasks, 216 correlated views, 300 named pairs and 1,944 owner claims remain planned. Eight families /144 views would be train and four /72 views exposed development. All four strata appear on development, so this is not sealed transfer. Existing RSM lineages0..11 remain separate. The proposed indices require an actual availability check before any production.

## Prospective graph sketches, not executed worlds

Each sketch would be assembled as a fully specified acyclic two-to-four-option program. Every sketch must retain the simultaneous common kernel: successful root/child entry, a nonzero committed effect, child initiation and selected-branch guard becoming false, an active saved register-derived argument subsequently consumed, and an advanced caller with pending work. These requirements cannot be satisfied by separate unrelated examples or by adding unused padding. The distinctions below require actual full and both coarse normalized graph checks before counting a family. Constants, names, cut locations and field ordering add no novelty.

| Index / split | Stratum | Proposed ordered dependency shape |
|---|---|---|
|12 /train|saved argument|Child branch adds to x before saved argument adds to y; child then calls leaf; root scales y and invokes leaf again. Three options, a shared leaf and nested conditional.|
|13 /train|saved argument|Root binds two register arguments; child changes x, exchanges registers and consumes both saved values on different writes; leaf is called once before a caller add. Two-argument def/use crosses a swap.|
|18 /dev|saved argument|Child captures a register and passes it to a leaf whose two ordered writes use that saved scalar; root has two child call sites separated by a scale. Repeated child call with a live inter-call read.|
|14 /train|caller continuation|Child invokes a grandchild from inside the selected branch, then root has a pending add followed by scale on the same register. Three option levels, one shared-register noncommuting caller chain.|
|15 /train|caller continuation|Root invokes two distinct children; the first contains a selected branch and leaf call, the second has two writes using the first child's result. Four options and sibling consumers.|
|19 /dev|caller continuation|Root invokes the same child twice; inside the child, two sequential leaf call sites surround a swap. Shared callee and repeated internal call; the root consumes the final result once.|
|16 /train|retained branch|A selected outer branch mutates its tested register, then selects an inner branch on the other register; leaf return precedes a parameter add. Nested different-register guard dependencies.|
|17 /train|retained branch|Child uses two consecutive conditionals rather than a nested conditional; first selected body reverses its guard, second reads a saved parameter and invokes leaf. Ordered sibling branches with different def/use edges.|
|28 /dev|retained branch|The chosen outer branch contains a call before an inner predicate whose register input is produced by the returned child; retained outer selection and return-dependent inner decision. Three levels, nested branch after callee completion.|
|20 /train|noncommuting calls|Root calls two different children in add-then-scale order on one register; the add child owns the retained branch. Distinct sibling callees with root exit depending on their shared output.|
|21 /train|noncommuting calls|Root reuses one child before and after swap; child scales one register then adds saved argument to the other. Cross-register caller swap changes the repeated call's live inputs.|
|29 /dev|noncommuting calls|A child calls two distinct leaves, one add and one scale, within its selected branch; root continuation writes the other register. Four options with three-level sibling leaves.|

The sketches deliberately vary ordered operator and call/branch/read/write shapes. They are hypotheses. Their execution feasibility, mutual distinctness and legacy noncollision are not established by this document or the hand calibration. If a sketch cannot satisfy all existing gates within24 declared instructions/48 events/depth3, replace it through a fresh source/config attempt without weakening the public rules. All produced traces must independently meet the public register/argument/option/branch/range bounds. These bounds constrain a future cohort; the general contract still uses exact unbounded integer arithmetic.

## Later construction sequence

1. After separate authorization, verify the actual index availability and freeze a new construction source/config. Instantiate F and validate all root/child/branch/argument dependencies and full trace bounds. Assign the source lineage/split before any rendering.
2. Calculate all three public signatures for every actual F graph. Compare the full-option, all-declared shared-primitive and entry-reachable shared-primitive views across12 programs and the actual frozen target-free legacy inputs. Alpha-canonicalize option/parameter/register names; erase literals, state, history/cuts/budgets/context/style while preserving ordered operators, comparisons and dependencies. Full view additionally retains guards/success and param/root structure; coarse views erase those wrappers and collapse constants/parameter operands to scalar operands. Actual legacy collapse counts and collisions are pending.
3. Choose an R boundary from the actual trace, not an assumed event index. Verify two active option frames and selected branch plus consequential immutable binding, root re-entry pass, child current initiation false and branch current guard false. Require a committed non-idempotent effect that T actually reexecutes, and a measured trace and state/status difference.
4. Derive T by mode only; P by budget only; U by actual extended history/second snapshot. Require P to commit a new effect and leave future semantic work. Derive A and I by exactly one root argument expression, with actual parameter use and sole-atom I failure. Derive S by one root-success literal, preserving effects and changing exit success only. Derive X by one active state/cursor/binding scalar with unchanged history; rotate tamper categories and prove each activity through a separate trusted-machine diagnostic while solve still rejects X.
5. Compute targets from the independently authored producer reference and separately verify actual programs with the other independently staffed implementation at integration. Retain any first failure; do not label a rejected relation as achieved. Difficulty2..4 requires a nonempty actual dependency explanation; depth/horizon/context alone never assigns a level.
6. Render complete public contract plus `Task JSON:\n` and exact F/R/T/P/U/A/I/S/X tasks. Reverse object keys recursively while keeping all lists/scalars identical. No context/source/difficulty/property/status metadata enters the prompt. Decode both actual prompts and compare typed tasks and targets.
7. Use the established canonical record API only in a later authorized runner. Each of216 records has exactly nine claims in `family_control_claims`, with at least one equal name/property identifier, literal name/scope, nonempty control_id, safe external evidence path and ordered actual pair IDs. Claims may describe family relations whose owner is a nonmember. Never put pair_record_hashes or a manifest hash inside canonical claims. Hash records first, freeze candidate bytes, then construct the external manifest with actual whole-candidate SHA, six operative raw public source bindings and the300 named actual pair entries. Any optional external member hashes consume already frozen records and must match them.
8. Publish actual per-family/context/split status/effect/horizon/frame/parameter histograms, exact controls/typed diffs/replay/caller/branch/tamper evidence and mode/history-length/majority/finite-program baselines. Preserve finite-program memorization and exposed-dev limitations. Prediction-before-gold expert/red reviews and later adjudication/release/remote custody remain separate gates.

## Separation and disclosure

Reference and handmade tests here are original producer work from public semantics. A separate direct recursive fresh simulator in the calibration source checks arithmetic/event traces without the reference's checkpoint/frame routines; it shares this author and model, so it is not an independent review role. The exact manually written19-event fixture and first/second snapshots are frozen before either implementation is imported or tested. Own calibration success establishes only those tested boundaries and mutations. No official oracle427, cohort checker, private fixture, review result or root crosscheck is read.

The author has substantial earlier producer context on RGM, belief and memory, and common model/root guidance with other roles. Those correlations can yield shared errors. Public design and potential later adjudication overlap is disclosed. Hand example reuse would require explicit disclosure and would still add no novelty. No option discovery, learned hierarchy, general retry, rollback, recursion, concurrency, policy selection, model training or efficacy is claimed.
